{"data": {"id": 131369, "accession_number": "1954.147", "share_license_status": "CC0", "tombstone": "Pendant (Hei-tiki), 1800s. Polynesia, New Zealand, Maori people, 19th century. Nephrite (greenstone); overall: 11.2 x 5.5 cm (4 7/16 x 2 3/16 in.). The Cleveland Museum of Art, John L. Severance Fund, 1954.147", "current_location": null, "title": "Pendant (Hei-tiki)", "creation_date": "1800s", "creation_date_earliest": 1800, "creation_date_latest": 1899, "artists_tags": [], "culture": ["Polynesia, New Zealand, Maori people, 19th century"], "technique": "nephrite (greenstone)", "support_materials": [], "department": "Oceania", "collection": "Oceanic Art", "type": "Sculpture", "measurements": "Overall: 11.2 x 5.5 cm (4 7/16 x 2 3/16 in.)", "dimensions": {"overall": {"height": 0.112, "height_inch": 4, "height_inch_fraction": 0.4375, "width": 0.055, "width_inch": 2, "width_inch_fraction": 0.1875}}, "state_of_the_work": null, "edition_of_the_work": null, "copyright": null, "inscriptions": [], "exhibitions": {"current": [], "legacy": []}, "provenance": [], "find_spot": null, "related_works": [], "former_accession_numbers": [], "did_you_know": "Hei-tiki may represent Hine-te-Iwaiwa, a legendary ancestress who is the exemplar of Māori womanhood.", "early_education_description": null, "artlens_description": null, "description": "Among the Māori, leaders are hereditary and imbued with mana, power and prestige that can be embodied and passed down in the artworks associated with them. Hei-tiki are among these treasured, mana-charged heirlooms, which connect the living to ancestors of the islands’ pre-European past. They may represent Hine-te-Iwaiwa, a legendary ancestress who is the exemplar of Māori womanhood and the patron of childbirth.", "external_resources": {"wikidata": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q80022613"], "internet_archive": ["https://archive.org/details/clevelandart-1954.147-pendant-hei-tiki"]}, "citations": [{"citation": "Frost, Natasha. \"He Calls the Tie a 'Co